          "text": "{\"category\":\"LVDS signal-path dropout affecting channels 32/33\",\"cause\":\"Unknown; the leading hypothesis is a disconnected, poorly seated, or failed LVDS pin/cable/driver path at signal pin 16.\",\"action\":\"With the run stopped, verify the pin16-to-channels32/33 mapping and whether these channels are intentionally disabled; then inspect and reseat the associated LVDS pins, jumper wires, and signal connections at both ends. Start a new diagnostic run and compare raw channel presence and pin16 counts. If pin16 remains zero, use a controlled cable/input swap or continuity test to distinguish cabling from board electronics; replace or repair the identified component conditionally. Do not unmask channels 32/33 until their masking rationale is confirmed.\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"supporting_historical_runs\":[2068,2071,2126],\"reasoning_summary\":\"OBSERVED: LVDS pin16 is persistently zero/abnormally low and maps exactly to channels 32/33, which are repeatedly reported missing and have waveform-feature anomalies; other signal pins have substantial counts. The overall trigger rate remains stable, while channels 32/33 are masked in the static configuration. INFERRED: this strongly localizes the problem to one LVDS pair rather than a detector-wide trigger or DAQ-rate failure. UNKNOWN: whether the zero began before the run, whether masking is intentional, and whether the fault is in cabling, connectors, or electronics; board matching and queue telemetry are unavailable. No historical case is an exact match. Runs 2068, 2071, and 2126 provide partial analogies involving degraded LVDS connections, but those cases included rate or matching behavior not established here.\"}"
